256
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:34,100
Exactly.

257
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:39,160
And that transition is perfect because it brings up a huge, glaring question for me.

258
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:43,200
We obviously have to freeze things sometimes to make our daily lives function, right?

259
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,960
Like, I don't calculate the gravitational pull of Jupiter when I'm trying to catch a

260
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:47,780
baseball in the park.

261
00:11:48,020 --> 00:11:48,840
No, that would be absurd.

262
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:54,380
So, how do we know when freezing a variable is a catastrophic mistake, like the red giant

263
00:11:54,380 --> 00:12:01,100
scenario, versus a totally necessary shortcut, like the ocean evaporation scenario?

264
00:12:01,380 --> 00:12:06,380
That is the exact question Flyxion answers in chapter one, with what they call the timescale

265
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:06,820
ratio.

266
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:07,120
Okay.

267
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:08,280
The timescale ratio.

268
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:11,500
It's a mathematical concept denoted by the Greek letter epsilon.

269
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:12,280
Epsilon.

270
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:12,980
Got it.

271
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:17,520
This ratio is the characteristic timescale of the phenomenon you were trying to predict,

272
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:23,060
divided by the timescale on which the background variable actually changes in the real world.

273
00:12:23,140 --> 00:12:23,320
Okay.

274
00:12:23,380 --> 00:12:25,960
Let's break that down into plain English for everyone listening.

275
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:27,140
Think of it as a fraction.

276
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:30,720
The numerator, the top number, is your prediction horizon.

277
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:32,900
How far out into the future are you looking?

278
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:34,160
Ten years.

279
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:34,560
Yeah.

280
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:35,400
A million years.

281
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:35,720
Okay.

282
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:37,620
The horizon is the top number.

283
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:37,920
Right.

284
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:42,980
And the denominator, the bottom number, is the amount of time it takes for your background

285
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:45,280
variable to undergo a significant change.

286
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:45,520
Right.

287
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:51,040
If you divide your prediction horizon by the variable's timescale, and the result, your

288
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:56,760
epsilon is a tiny fraction, like much, much less than the number one, then freezing that

289
00:12:56,760 --> 00:12:58,580
variable is a legitimate simplification.

290
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:02,920
Meaning, you can safely ignore it, pretend it doesn't move, and your model will still

291
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:03,880
work perfectly fine.

292
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:04,420
Exactly.

293
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:07,620
Flyxion gives some wonderfully grounded examples of this.

294
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:10,740
Think about the engineers who designed the Golden Gate Bridge.

295
00:13:10,820 --> 00:13:11,060
Okay.

296
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:13,960
They completely ignored continental drift.

297
00:13:14,280 --> 00:13:14,520
Right.

298
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:16,180
Or airplane designers.

299
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:21,760
They do not factor in stellar evolution when they build a Boeing 777.

300
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:22,360
Yeah.

301
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:27,240
Nobody re-derives orbital mechanics to compute a 30-year mortgage amortization schedule.

302
00:13:27,340 --> 00:13:27,900
No, exactly.

303
00:13:28,100 --> 00:13:28,320
Right.

304
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:33,440
If I go to my bank to buy a house, and the loan officer says, well, the tectonic plates

305
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:37,740
are shifting about two inches a year, so your interest rate might fluctuate by a millimeter

306
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:41,140
over the next three decades, I mean, I'm finding a new bank.

307
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:48,360
Because in those cases, the frozen quantity changes on a timescale wildly longer than the

308
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:49,200
thing being predicted.

309
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:51,200
The engineers aren't being careless.

310
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:54,660
That omission is the entire point of mathematical modeling.

311
00:13:55,080 --> 00:13:55,580
Precisely.

312
00:13:55,980 --> 00:14:01,140
A model that allowed every single variable in the entire universe to move simultaneously

313
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:02,560
wouldn't be a tool.

314
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:05,860
It would be a one-to-one perfect simulation of reality.

315
00:14:05,940 --> 00:14:06,500
Like the matrix.

316
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:07,040
Right.

317
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:12,240
And a one-to-one simulation is completely useless for answering a specific bounded question.

318
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:13,660
You'd be overwhelmed with data.

319
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:18,780
So wait, if I'm understanding this timescale ratio correctly, um, sorry, let me rephrase.

320
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:26,480
The same exact variable, the same physical object can be a totally legitimate simplification

321
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:32,420
on Tuesday and a catastrophic frozen process error on Wednesday, depending purely on how far

322
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:33,120
out I'm trying to look.

323
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:33,520
Yes.

324
00:14:33,800 --> 00:14:36,020
You have hit the absolute core of the theory right there.

325
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:39,320
The ratio is not a fixed property of the variable itself.

326
00:14:39,480 --> 00:14:39,740
Okay.

327
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:43,220
A rock is not inherently a static background object.

328
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:46,480
The ratio is entirely dependent on the horizon of your question.

329
00:14:46,640 --> 00:14:47,580
Give me an example of that.

330
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:49,880
Let's look at the climate example Flyxion provides.

331
00:14:50,700 --> 00:14:53,780
Imagine you are forecasting Earth's climate over the next century.

332
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:54,620
A hundred years.

333
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:55,280
Got it.

334
00:14:55,280 --> 00:15:01,860
Over a hundred years, things like ocean circulation patterns, ice sheet extent, and global vegetation

335
00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:04,760
cover are all dynamic state variables.

336
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:06,120
They are moving.

337
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:06,980
They are fluctuating.

338
00:15:07,060 --> 00:15:07,400
Sure.

339
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:13,260
But the physical positions of the continents, they're moving so slowly that you can safely

340
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:16,660
park them in your parameter set as a fixed background.

341
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:20,060
Your epsilon is incredibly close to zero.

342
00:15:20,380 --> 00:15:24,820
Because North America moves maybe a few feet over a century, that is totally irrelevant

343
00:15:24,820 --> 00:15:27,700
to predicting the hurricane season in Florida next year.

344
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:28,240
Exactly.

345
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:28,720
Yeah.

346
00:15:28,860 --> 00:15:30,940
But now, extend your prediction horizon.

347
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:35,420
Let's say you want to model the Earth's climate over tens of millions of years.

348
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:35,860
Okay.

349
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:36,860
Huge jump.

350
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:41,740
Suddenly, continental drift, which operates on a timescale about 10 million years, has

351
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:43,380
an epsilon ratio approaching one.

352
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:46,920
Oh, because the top number and the bottom number of the fraction are almost the same

353
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:47,160
now.

354
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:47,520
Yes.

355
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:52,320
It is now changing on a timescale entirely comparable to your prediction horizon.

356
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:56,740
The approximation weakens, and eventually, the math collapses entirely.

357
00:15:57,140 --> 00:16:03,940
Over tens of millions of years, continental drift must become a dynamic state variable in

358
00:16:03,940 --> 00:16:04,400
your model.

359
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:10,060
Well, the boundary between what is background scenery and what is an active participant literally

360
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:10,680
shifts.

361
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:12,140
That is wild.

362
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:17,980
The exact same piece of reality, the physical mass of a continent, crosses the line from

363
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:22,220
a painting on the wall to an actor on the stage, just because I let the stopwatch run

364
00:16:22,220 --> 00:16:22,660
longer.

365
00:16:22,860 --> 00:16:24,300
It's a great way to visualize it.

366
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:24,600
Yeah.

367
00:16:24,600 --> 00:16:29,760
And Flyxion argues that whenever you build a model or make a life forecast, you have to

368
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:30,860
run this timescale check.

369
00:16:31,020 --> 00:16:32,180
You just have to make it a habit.

370
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,160
You have to ask.

371
00:16:33,160 --> 00:16:38,080
For every variable I am holding constant, how quickly does it actually change?

372
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:43,160
And critically, is the frozen quantity downstream of anything already active in my model?

373
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:45,460
Wait, give me an example of a downstream variable.

374
00:16:45,560 --> 00:16:48,300
Well, the Earth's orbit from our first example.

375
00:16:48,380 --> 00:16:49,040
Oh, right.

376
00:16:49,060 --> 00:16:51,500
The orbital radius isn't just floating out there independently.

377
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:54,200
It is functionally dependent on the sun's mass.

378
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:54,620
Okay.

379
00:16:54,620 --> 00:16:58,980
And the sun's mass is already the primary active variable the scientists were tracking.

380
00:16:59,380 --> 00:17:00,360
Ah, I see.

381
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:06,860
To track the sun's mass loss, but freeze the orbit, is a frozen process error by construction.

382
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:12,380
The model already contained all the mathematical information needed to unfreeze the Earth.

383
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:15,180
The scientists just refused to make the connection.

384
00:17:15,180 --> 00:17:23,340
It's like tracking the exact amount of rain falling into a plastic bucket, but treating the overall weight of the bucket as a fixed constant.

385
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:24,720
Yes, perfect analogy.

386
00:17:24,880 --> 00:17:30,880
You literally have the data telling you the bucket is taking on water, but you just aren't hooking those two concepts together.

387
00:17:31,100 --> 00:17:31,540
Exactly.

388
00:17:32,080 --> 00:17:33,440
Contrast that with the bridge engineer.

389
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,900
Continental drift is not caused by the stress of cars driving on the bridge.

390
00:17:38,020 --> 00:17:38,220
Right.

391
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:44,300
The complete physical independence of those variables is what makes freezing the tectonic plates legitimate.

392
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:49,640
Which makes me realize the real danger here isn't just doing bad math in a spreadsheet.

393
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:54,000
The danger is that we don't even realize we've made these categorizations in our heads.

394
00:17:54,140 --> 00:17:55,720
We're completely blind to it.

395
00:17:55,820 --> 00:17:56,060
Right.

396
00:17:56,220 --> 00:18:01,840
We don't wake up in the morning and consciously say, I am now choosing to freeze the concept of my marriage.

397
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:03,480
We just do it by default.

398
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:04,240
Yeah, it's automatic.

399
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:09,520
And this moves us perfectly from the hard physics into the deep philosophy of modeling.

400
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:14,240
Flyxion calls this the ghost in the math prediction as ontology.

401
00:18:15,380 --> 00:18:17,640
The state parameter misclassification principle.

402
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:19,200
That's a mouthful.

403
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:20,940
It is, but it's essential.

404
00:18:21,900 --> 00:18:33,860
Every single forecast you make, whether it's a supercomputer running climate models or you just guessing what time you need to leave for work to beat traffic, begins with a fundamental partition of the world.

405
00:18:33,860 --> 00:18:34,660
A partition.

406
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:38,180
You divide reality into things that act.

407
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:45,100
These are your state variables, denoted mathematically as S, and things that simply are these your parameters, denoted as theta.

408
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:47,320
Things that act and things that simply are.

409
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:47,660
Okay.

410
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:59,480
Flyxion argues that a frozen process error happens when a quantity is thrown into the parameter bucket because it is conceived of as a fixed characteristic of the system, when in reality its epsilon ratio is not small.

411
00:18:59,580 --> 00:19:01,780
It's actively changing out there in the dark.

412
00:19:02,180 --> 00:19:02,660
Precisely.

413
00:19:02,740 --> 00:19:05,180
Okay, here's where it gets really interesting, at least to me.

414
00:19:05,540 --> 00:19:09,240
Flyxion says these errors are actually invisible from inside the model.

415
00:19:09,460 --> 00:19:10,580
They are completely invisible.

416
00:19:10,580 --> 00:19:19,840
But if I build a bad model of my commute, can't I just look at the times I was late, see my model was wrong, and tweak the math until it's right?

417
00:19:20,340 --> 00:19:23,500
How on earth do we ever spot this error if it's invisible?

418
00:19:23,980 --> 00:19:27,860
You cannot fix an ontological error by running the math more accurately.

419
00:19:28,080 --> 00:19:28,600
Why not?

420
00:19:28,600 --> 00:19:35,500
Because the variable that would reveal your mistake is categorically absent from the equations you wrote down.

421
00:19:35,620 --> 00:19:36,480
Oh, wow.

422
00:19:36,700 --> 00:19:43,180
Let's say your equation is calculating the future based on your active variables, the S, and your fixed parameters, the theta.

423
00:19:43,400 --> 00:19:43,620
Okay.

424
00:19:43,620 --> 00:19:46,440
The calculation itself might be flawlessly executed.

425
00:19:47,020 --> 00:19:50,900
A supercomputer might run it a billion times without a single arithmetic error.

426
00:19:51,580 --> 00:19:54,240
It is perfectly correct based on its own assumptions.

427
00:19:54,520 --> 00:19:55,960
But the assumptions are wrong.

428
00:19:55,960 --> 00:19:56,380
Right.

429
00:19:56,540 --> 00:20:07,320
The true trajectory of reality diverges from your model because your parameter, theta, is actually moving out there in the real world, and your computer doesn't even have a column in its database for that movement.

430
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:10,400
So throwing a faster processor at the problem doesn't help at all.

431
00:20:10,600 --> 00:20:11,300
Not even a little.

432
00:20:11,800 --> 00:20:18,320
If a model treats biological species as fixed, immutable types, it cannot represent evolutionary change.

433
00:20:18,320 --> 00:20:25,500
Not because it is signed as zero to a change variable, but because it contains no change variable to begin with.

434
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:27,600
The error isn't in the equations.

435
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:29,260
The error is in the ontology.

436
00:20:29,380 --> 00:20:31,040
The equations were built to express.

437
00:20:31,400 --> 00:20:31,880
Ontology.

438
00:20:31,980 --> 00:20:38,860
Let's define that for everyone because it's a word that gets thrown around in philosophy 101, but it is the absolute linchpin of this entire discussion.

439
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:43,500
Yes, ontology is the philosophical study of being or reality.

440
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:46,260
It asks, what is?

441
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:47,060
What exists?

442
00:20:47,320 --> 00:20:53,600
In forecasting, your ontology dictates your fundamental assumptions about what kinds of things exist in your system.

443
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,180
Are they actors or are they the stage?

444
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:56,620
Okay.

445
00:20:56,860 --> 00:21:01,180
A forecaster who suspects a variable might be moving will try to model its dynamics.

446
00:21:01,380 --> 00:21:01,620
Yeah.

447
00:21:01,680 --> 00:21:07,880
But a forecaster who has ontologically classified something as a fixed background property never even runs the timescale check.

448
00:21:07,880 --> 00:21:09,260
Because it wouldn't even occur to them.

449
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:09,760
Exactly.

450
00:21:10,160 --> 00:21:12,380
The question, how fast does this change?

451
00:21:12,560 --> 00:21:12,720
Yeah.

452
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:15,180
Is simply not the kind of question you ask about a constant.

453
00:21:15,420 --> 00:21:23,020
You don't ask a physicist how fast the speed of light is changing because by ontological definition, the speed of light is a constant.

454
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:24,080
Exactly.

455
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:29,760
If I can try to apply this to everyday life for you listening, think about a struggling marriage.

456
00:21:30,260 --> 00:21:34,420
One partner might say, our relationship is failing because we don't spend enough time together.

457
00:21:34,420 --> 00:21:35,540
Let's run the math.

458
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:37,680
Let's schedule date nights on Thursdays.

459
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:39,520
We tweak the act of variables.

460
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:40,000
Right.

461
00:21:40,060 --> 00:21:41,120
They try to fix the schedule.

462
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:47,100
But what if the problem is that the fundamental nature of what both people want out of life has drifted over 10 years?

463
00:21:47,880 --> 00:21:49,480
The schedule is just a math problem.

464
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:54,540
The shifting foundation of their desires is an ontological problem.

465
00:21:54,680 --> 00:21:54,900
Yes.

466
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:58,380
If you only fix the schedule, you're missing the ghost in the math.

467
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:00,440
That is a phenomenal application.

468
00:22:00,440 --> 00:22:04,620
And it produces a very specific diagnostic signature.

469
00:22:04,820 --> 00:22:05,340
Which is?

470
00:22:05,400 --> 00:22:09,400
These errors survive massive upgrades in effort and technology.

471
00:22:09,520 --> 00:22:11,300
Because you're working harder on the wrong thing.

472
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:11,940
Exactly.

473
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:16,640
The couple can schedule 50 dates perfectly and the marriage still fails.

474
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:22,300
The astrophysicist can refine their model of stellar expansion to the decimal point.

475
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:28,320
And they will still be wrong about the Earth's fate because those refinements never touch the fixed parameters.

476
00:22:28,820 --> 00:22:31,120
So to fix it, you need an ontological shift.

477
00:22:31,200 --> 00:22:33,720
You have to ask a question that wasn't being asked before.

478
00:22:33,940 --> 00:22:34,320
Yes.

479
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:39,260
You have to fundamentally change your mind about what kind of universe you live in.

480
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:41,100
Which sounds incredibly hard to do.

481
00:22:41,200 --> 00:22:44,460
I mean, I don't want to change my fundamental view of reality on a Tuesday.

482
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:45,820
Very few people do.

483
00:22:45,820 --> 00:22:53,780
And Flyxion proves how hard it is in Chapter 4, which is basically a tour through human history as a graveyard of frozen parameters.

484
00:22:54,700 --> 00:22:57,160
Humanity has repeatedly fallen into this trap.

485
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:03,980
And it has required massive, painful, multi-generational scientific revolutions just to unfreeze our parameters.

486
00:23:04,180 --> 00:23:06,960
This is one of the most compelling syntheses in the monograph.

487
00:23:06,980 --> 00:23:07,900
The historical principle.

488
00:23:07,900 --> 00:23:08,520
Let's get into it.

489
00:23:08,520 --> 00:23:19,700
Flyxion argues that what looks from inside a paradigm, like the brilliant discovery of a new phenomenon, is often from outside it, just the mundane correction of a frozen process error.

490
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:23,800
Okay, let's walk through the deep dives they provide, starting with biology.

491
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:24,680
Perfect.

492
00:23:25,020 --> 00:23:25,800
Let's look at Darwin.

493
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,300
Darwin, the ultimate paradigm shifter.

494
00:23:28,300 --> 00:23:34,560
For most of the history of natural science, species were the paradigm case of a fixed parameter, a theta.

495
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:37,240
A species was a kind.

496
00:23:37,820 --> 00:23:38,680
It was defined.

497
00:23:38,820 --> 00:23:40,120
It was completely stable.

498
00:23:40,780 --> 00:23:49,140
If you were a naturalist in 1700, you collected a specimen, you assigned it to a category, and that category was not considered a variable.

499
00:23:49,300 --> 00:23:51,140
It was just a box you put the animal in.

500
00:23:51,140 --> 00:24:05,040
The ontology was purely essentialist, meaning species had defining natures given by a creator, and individual variation, like a bird with a slightly longer beak or a moth with darker wings, was just noise around a fixed perfect type.

501
00:24:05,100 --> 00:24:07,640
Wait, so they just thought a finch was a finch?

502
00:24:07,920 --> 00:24:08,380
Exactly.

503
00:24:08,520 --> 00:24:09,660
They thought it was entirely fixed.

504
00:24:09,780 --> 00:24:10,460
That's wild.

505
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:12,920
So what did Darwin actually do in this framework?

506
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:17,880
What Charles Darwin did was move species from the fixed theta bucket to the active state S bucket.

507
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:18,800
The unfroze it.

508
00:24:18,800 --> 00:24:20,800
He didn't just discover new bones.

509
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:30,120
He realized that what had been a static classification was actually a state with its own dynamics, driven by heritable variation and differential reproduction.

510
00:24:30,500 --> 00:24:34,580
And if we apply the timescale ratio to Darwin, how does that work out?

511
00:24:34,700 --> 00:24:35,680
It maps perfectly.

512
00:24:35,680 --> 00:24:42,340
The timescale of species change is the generation time multiplied by the evolutionary selection pressure.

513
00:24:42,340 --> 00:24:46,460
For bacteria under antibiotics, that timescale is years.

514
00:24:46,940 --> 00:24:51,320
For slowly evolving lineages, it's millions of years.

515
00:24:51,540 --> 00:24:51,640
Right.

516
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:59,680
If you are a medieval monk doing a human-scale natural history study of the birds in your garden, your prediction horizon is maybe 10 years.

517
00:25:00,080 --> 00:25:05,520
Your epsilon is tiny, and fixing the species as a constant is a totally legitimate shortcut.

518
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:09,640
Because the birds aren't going to turn into a new species while you're tending your garden.

519
00:25:09,640 --> 00:25:10,240
Exactly.

520
00:25:10,540 --> 00:25:13,860
But against a geological timescale, epsilon approaches one.

521
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:20,420
The fixity assumption produces a worldview of fixed types whose sheer diversity simply cannot be explained.

522
00:25:20,840 --> 00:25:22,920
So Darwin didn't just find new data.

523
00:25:23,080 --> 00:25:24,260
He unfroze the parameter.

524
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:24,620
Exactly.

525
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:25,620
That is so cool.

526
00:25:25,700 --> 00:25:27,120
Okay, let's do geology next.

527
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:29,560
Alfred Wegener and plate tectonics.

528
00:25:29,560 --> 00:25:37,120
This one always fascinated me because people genuinely thought Wegener was a crackpot when he first suggested it in 1912.

529
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:38,160
Oh, they ridiculed him.

530
00:25:38,260 --> 00:25:41,360
And Flixute explains exactly why they thought he was a crackpot.

531
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:41,860
Why?

532
00:25:42,060 --> 00:25:46,880
For virtually all of recorded science, continental positions were background conditions.

533
00:25:47,620 --> 00:25:53,520
A geologist in 1900 did not sit down and try to mathematically model the physical movement of South America.

534
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:53,920
Right.

535
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:59,580
South America was the fixed stage on which geology like earthquakes and volcanoes happened.

536
00:25:59,680 --> 00:26:01,400
It was the theater, not the actors.

537
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:01,920
Exactly.

538
00:26:02,480 --> 00:26:08,540
When Wegener proposed that the continents had actually drifted apart over time, it was vehemently rejected.

539
00:26:09,020 --> 00:26:11,500
Partly because he lacked a mechanism for how they moved.

540
00:26:11,500 --> 00:26:24,240
But fundamentally because the very idea of modeling continents as state variables, treating massive landmasses as things with rates of change, was categorically foreign to the established ontology of geology.

541
00:26:24,380 --> 00:26:27,340
They literally couldn't conceive of a continent as a moving object.

542
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:29,980
It broke their mental model of reality.

543
00:26:30,220 --> 00:26:36,760
If you told a geologist in 1920 that Africa was moving, it would be like telling me today that the color blue is moving.

544
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:38,200
It just doesn't compute.

545
00:26:38,360 --> 00:26:38,520
Right.

546
00:26:38,600 --> 00:26:39,880
It's a category error to them.

547
00:26:39,940 --> 00:26:40,120
Yeah.

548
00:26:40,120 --> 00:26:43,880
Until plate tectonics finally resolved it in the 1960s.

549
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:45,460
Which took 50 years.

550
00:26:45,620 --> 00:26:50,020
Because the timescale of continental motion is tens of millions of years.

551
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:54,120
Against the human history horizon, epsilon is tiny.

552
00:26:54,620 --> 00:26:59,440
We do not calculate continental drift when we use Google Maps to drive to the grocery store.

553
00:26:59,620 --> 00:26:59,640
Right.

554
00:26:59,780 --> 00:27:00,160
Obviously.

555
00:27:00,500 --> 00:27:03,360
But against geological horizons, epsilon is massive.

556
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:10,160
If you treat continents as fixed over millions of years, you end up with systematic, unsolvable errors.

557
00:27:10,320 --> 00:27:11,540
Like what kind of errors?

558
00:27:12,080 --> 00:27:21,860
You find identical fossils of ancient reptiles on continents separated by vast oceans, or you find inexplicable patterns of mountain building that don't make sense on a static map.

559
00:27:21,860 --> 00:27:24,580
And unfreezing the map solved all those anomalies.

560
00:27:24,740 --> 00:27:25,220
Instantly.

561
00:27:25,220 --> 00:27:28,460
And the third historical example Flyxion gives is cosmology.

562
00:27:28,960 --> 00:27:31,460
Edwin Hubble unfreezing the universe itself.

563
00:27:31,460 --> 00:27:33,400
This is the ultimate unfreezing.

564
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:37,640
Before Hubble in the 1920s, the universe was merely the container.

565
00:27:38,220 --> 00:27:38,980
Stars moved.

566
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:40,500
Galaxies might rotate.

567
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:45,060
But the universe itself, the fabric of space, was the fixed background.

568
00:27:45,560 --> 00:27:47,000
It was not a dynamical object.

569
00:27:47,220 --> 00:27:47,420
Okay.

570
00:27:47,560 --> 00:27:55,000
Hubble's discovery of the expansion of the universe moved the universe's scale factor from a fixed parameter to a dynamic state variable.

571
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,100
The container became a participant.

572
00:27:57,500 --> 00:27:58,160
Beautifully put.

573
00:27:58,760 --> 00:28:06,760
And subsequent discoveries, like dark energy accelerating that expansion, have made the universe's own dynamics just as complicated as anything happening inside it.

574
00:28:06,840 --> 00:28:07,120
Wow.

575
00:28:07,400 --> 00:28:14,240
What is notable here is that the fractional change in the universe's expansion over a single human lifetime is basically unmeasurable.

576
00:28:14,980 --> 00:28:17,620
The time scale is unimaginably enormous.

577
00:28:17,780 --> 00:28:17,980
Right.

578
00:28:18,100 --> 00:28:20,800
Our epsilons are practically zero for everyday life.

579
00:28:20,800 --> 00:28:24,720
Yet for cosmological questions about the origin of light, epsilon is near one.

580
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:33,620
If you assume a fixed universe, you get systematic errors in calculating the age of stars, the distances between galaxies, and the interpretation of light redshift.

581
00:28:33,980 --> 00:28:36,360
So, synthesizing all of this.

582
00:28:37,480 --> 00:28:43,200
A scientific revolution usually isn't just the discovery of a new shiny object out there in the dark.

583
00:28:43,860 --> 00:28:50,240
It is the realization that something we thought was a background painting is actually an actor walking around on the stage.

584
00:28:50,480 --> 00:28:51,560
That's a perfect summary.

585
00:28:51,560 --> 00:28:59,820
It is taking something that simply was and recognizing it as something that changes according to its own internal dynamics.

586
00:29:00,260 --> 00:29:02,160
That is the historical principle in a nutshell.

587
00:29:02,980 --> 00:29:09,540
We advance as a species by repeatedly correcting these errors by moving quantities from the parameter set to the state set.

588
00:29:09,700 --> 00:29:11,300
Okay, but I want to push back on this a little.

589
00:29:11,440 --> 00:29:11,600
Sure.

590
00:29:11,600 --> 00:29:17,660
All of these historical examples, birds, rocks, stars, the universe itself, these are physical things.

591
00:29:18,140 --> 00:29:20,700
They follow the rigid laws of physics and biology.

592
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:21,140
True.

593
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:24,220
What happens when the thing we are freezing isn't made of atoms?

594
00:29:24,780 --> 00:29:27,560
What happens when the variable we are freezing is human behavior?

595
00:29:27,880 --> 00:29:32,300
Because humans are notoriously uncooperative when you try to put them in a mathematical spreadsheet.

596
00:29:32,300 --> 00:29:39,040
And this brings us to what Flyxion calls level two in the hierarchy of freezing, the Lucas Principle and adaptive systems.

597
00:29:39,140 --> 00:29:39,960
Okay, level two.

598
00:29:40,120 --> 00:29:42,280
It is one thing to freeze a physical process.

599
00:29:43,060 --> 00:29:46,440
It is another entirely to freeze a behavioral rule.

600
00:29:46,660 --> 00:29:49,140
Why is behavior harder to map than rocks?

601
00:29:50,140 --> 00:29:56,900
Because a behavioral rule, the way human agents respond to their environment, might actually have been perfectly constant in the past.

602
00:29:56,960 --> 00:29:57,240
Right.

603
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:00,140
It might be entirely historically defensible to freeze it.

604
00:30:00,540 --> 00:30:02,660
You might have 50 years of data proving it works.

605
00:30:02,820 --> 00:30:03,800
So it looks solid.

606
00:30:04,040 --> 00:30:08,500
But it becomes systematically non-constant the exact moment your model is acted upon.

607
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:09,100
Wait, what?

608
00:30:09,300 --> 00:30:11,900
Your forecast doesn't just fail to capture the behavior.

609
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:18,200
The very act of publishing your forecast actually changes the behavior it was meant to predict.

610
00:30:18,460 --> 00:30:19,020
Oh, wow.

611
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:21,040
Okay, walk us through the Lucas Critique.

612
00:30:21,140 --> 00:30:23,460
This is from Macri Economics in the 1970s, right?

613
00:30:23,460 --> 00:30:23,760
Yes.

614
00:30:23,760 --> 00:30:34,160
In 1976, Robert Lucas published a devastating critique of the large-scale macroeconomic models that governments and central banks were using to dictate national policy.

615
00:30:35,060 --> 00:30:40,180
These models operated by estimating stable statistical relationships between economic variables.

616
00:30:40,760 --> 00:30:45,800
The most famous example is the Phillips Curve, the historical relationship between inflation and unemployment.

617
00:30:45,800 --> 00:30:54,720
Meaning, economists looked at decades of data and said, hey, historically, when inflation goes up a little bit, unemployment tends to go down a little bit.

618
00:30:54,720 --> 00:30:55,280
Exactly.

619
00:30:55,540 --> 00:31:02,500
And they treated that relationship as a fixed parameter, a structural, unchangeable constant of the economy.

620
00:31:02,600 --> 00:31:03,180
They froze it.

621
00:31:03,180 --> 00:31:07,420
They built massive models and used them to predict policy effects.

622
00:31:07,420 --> 00:31:18,040
If the central bank intentionally raises the money supply by X amount to induce a little inflation, output and employment will reliably increase by Y amount.

623
00:31:18,220 --> 00:31:24,320
Which sounds perfectly logical if the past dictates the future, you pull lever A, and you get result B.

624
00:31:24,320 --> 00:31:29,860
But Lucas pointed out that these historical relationships are not structural constants like the speed of light.

625
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:31,680
They are behavioral rules.

626
00:31:32,300 --> 00:31:42,720
They are the equilibrium outcomes of millions of human beings, workers, factory owners, shoppers, optimizing their lives based on their expectations about government policy.

627
00:31:42,840 --> 00:31:43,960
Oh, I see where this is going.

628
00:31:44,180 --> 00:31:51,140
If the central bank suddenly changes its policy to exploit that historical relationship, rational human beings update their expectations.

629
00:31:51,140 --> 00:31:54,440
Let me try to put this in a real world scenario to make sure I'm following.

630
00:31:54,560 --> 00:31:54,780
What?

631
00:31:54,920 --> 00:31:55,360
Please do.

632
00:31:55,700 --> 00:31:58,260
Let's say I'm a factory worker in 1975.

633
00:31:59,040 --> 00:32:04,700
If I know the government is intentionally printing money to lower unemployment, I'm not just going to sit there.

634
00:32:04,840 --> 00:32:05,100
Right.

635
00:32:05,300 --> 00:32:07,520
I know prices at the grocery store are going to go up.

636
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:17,100
So I go to my boss or my union goes to the boss and we demand a cost of living raise before the inflation hits to compensate for what we know is coming.

637
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:17,680
Exactly.

638
00:32:17,920 --> 00:32:18,400
You adapt.

639
00:32:18,400 --> 00:32:23,820
And suddenly the government strategy stops working because the labor market adapted to the forecast.

640
00:32:24,100 --> 00:32:24,700
Precisely.

641
00:32:25,160 --> 00:32:26,160
The humans adapt.

642
00:32:26,780 --> 00:32:29,240
The estimated historical relationship breaks down.

643
00:32:29,880 --> 00:32:35,220
What the central bank's model placed in the fixed parameter bucket actually belonged in the dynamic state bucket.

644
00:32:35,480 --> 00:32:36,020
Wow.

645
00:32:36,340 --> 00:32:39,440
This is what Flyxion calls a feedback sensitive forecast.

646
00:32:40,120 --> 00:32:44,520
The behavioral parameter depends on the policy implied by the model's own output.

647
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:48,800
You cannot just run the old macroeconomic model more carefully to fix it.

648
00:32:49,020 --> 00:32:50,960
The model destroys its own foundation.

649
00:32:51,360 --> 00:32:56,000
It's like trying to perfectly map out a chess game assuming your opponent will never react to your moves.

650
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:56,960
That's a perfect analogy.

651
00:32:57,420 --> 00:33:02,660
You sit there and plan five brilliant moves ahead based on the current layout of the board.

652
00:33:02,660 --> 00:33:08,700
But the very act of you making your first move completely changes the landscape of the board.

653
00:33:09,080 --> 00:33:10,300
The opponent adapts.

654
00:33:10,700 --> 00:33:12,440
Your model is instantly useless.

655
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:16,620
And this phenomenon isn't just limited to 1970s macroeconomics.

656
00:33:17,520 --> 00:33:21,560
Flyxion applies this to adaptive markets in modern finance.

657
00:33:21,700 --> 00:33:23,120
Like Wall Street algorithms.

658
00:33:23,420 --> 00:33:23,820
Exactly.

659
00:33:24,380 --> 00:33:32,700
Imagine a quantitative hedge fund discovers a highly complex trading rule that generates abnormal returns based on some historical mispricing of a stock.

660
00:33:32,700 --> 00:33:34,440
They find a loophole in the market.

661
00:33:34,740 --> 00:33:36,020
They find a mathematical loophole.

662
00:33:36,780 --> 00:33:46,560
But the moment they start trading millions of dollars on it and the moment other market participants notice that volume and attract competing capital to that exact same strategy, the arbitrage is eliminated.

663
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:48,520
Oh, because everyone else starts doing it.

664
00:33:48,580 --> 00:33:48,760
Right.

665
00:33:49,020 --> 00:33:53,880
The asset values are bid up to exactly the levels the model predicts and the anomaly vanishes.

666
00:33:54,300 --> 00:33:57,380
The behavioral pattern erodes because it was recognized and exploited.

667
00:33:57,740 --> 00:34:01,860
So the timescale of adaptation here isn't millions of years like continental drift.

668
00:34:01,860 --> 00:34:02,460
No.

669
00:34:02,640 --> 00:34:09,980
In financial markets, the timescale of adaptation is months, sometimes days, and with algorithmic trading, milliseconds.

670
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:10,660
Jesus.

671
00:34:11,380 --> 00:34:16,060
Measured against a prediction horizon of a few years, the epsilon ratio is massive.

672
00:34:17,200 --> 00:34:22,600
Freezing the market's response to a new trading rule is a catastrophic frozen process error.

673
00:34:22,980 --> 00:34:28,580
Infliction also mentions ecology here, co-evolutionary systems, because animals adapt too.

674
00:34:28,800 --> 00:34:30,660
Think of predator and prey dynamics.

675
00:34:30,660 --> 00:34:41,560
If you build a conservation model to protect a specific species of prey and you treat the local predator's foraging pattern as a fixed parameter, your model will generate systematic errors.

676
00:34:41,820 --> 00:34:42,340
Why?

677
00:34:42,720 --> 00:34:51,280
Because over ecological time, just years or decades, the predator adapts its hunting behavior to the prey's defensive behavior, and the prey adapts to the predator.

678
00:34:51,440 --> 00:34:51,700
Right.

679
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:53,100
An evolutionary arms race.

680
00:34:53,100 --> 00:34:54,500
They are feedback sensitive.

681
00:34:55,160 --> 00:35:00,700
Fixing one while mathematically modeling the other is a frozen process error in an adaptive system.

682
00:35:00,920 --> 00:35:05,340
So we've seen how economists and finance bros and ecologists fall into this trap.

683
00:35:05,660 --> 00:35:12,020
But what's genuinely terrifying to me is that this psychological blindness to changing systems isn't just a quirk of bad math.

684
00:35:12,320 --> 00:35:13,560
No, it's much deeper than that.

685
00:35:13,560 --> 00:35:20,060
The reason we are so prone to freezing the world is actually baked into the very words coming out of our mouths right now.

686
00:35:21,100 --> 00:35:26,880
Think about basic grammar, which brings us to section six of the text, the noun fallacy.

687
00:35:26,880 --> 00:35:32,960
This is where the monograph moves beautifully from applied mathematics into deep linguistics and philosophy.

688
00:35:33,100 --> 00:35:33,960
I love this part.

689
00:35:33,960 --> 00:35:43,480
Flyxion proposes that the grammatical distinction between nouns and verbs encodes a massive, implicit, ontological presumption about how the universe works.

690
00:35:43,700 --> 00:35:43,940
Okay.

691
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:46,120
A noun is a person, place, or thing.

692
00:35:46,700 --> 00:35:48,140
A verb is an action.

693
00:35:48,580 --> 00:35:50,680
That's what we all learned in first grade grammar.

694
00:35:50,820 --> 00:35:52,120
A noun names a thing.

695
00:35:52,620 --> 00:35:57,320
And in our ordinary grammatical sense, things are entities that persist.

696
00:35:57,460 --> 00:35:58,560
They endure over time.

697
00:35:58,600 --> 00:35:59,380
You can point to them.

698
00:35:59,460 --> 00:35:59,680
Okay.

699
00:35:59,980 --> 00:36:02,860
Verbs or processes are events that happen to things.

700
00:36:02,860 --> 00:36:04,260
The noun is primary.

701
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:05,460
The verb is secondary.

702
00:36:06,100 --> 00:36:08,320
But Flyxion argues that this is fundamentally backwards.

703
00:36:09,040 --> 00:36:12,320
The natural order of the universe is not objects moving through time.

704
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:12,720
Okay.

705
00:36:12,820 --> 00:36:13,200
I'll bite.

706
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:15,400
If it's not objects moving through time, what is it?

707
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:17,480
The natural order is trajectories.

708
00:36:18,120 --> 00:36:21,740
Nouns are simply what we call trajectories that are stable enough to merit a name.

709
00:36:21,800 --> 00:36:22,020
Whoa.

710
00:36:22,160 --> 00:36:22,320
Okay.

711
00:36:22,420 --> 00:36:22,600
Wait.

712
00:36:23,520 --> 00:36:25,340
Trajectories stable enough to merit a name.

713
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:26,200
Let's unpack that.

714
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:28,440
Because if I pick up a rock, that rock is a noun.

715
00:36:28,580 --> 00:36:29,260
It is a thing.

716
00:36:29,380 --> 00:36:30,340
It is not a trajectory.

717
00:36:30,600 --> 00:36:31,200
But it is.

718
00:36:31,660 --> 00:36:32,560
Think about the earth.

719
00:36:32,860 --> 00:36:34,220
From our astrophysics example.

720
00:36:34,760 --> 00:36:38,620
Earth is not a static rock moving through time.

721
00:36:38,940 --> 00:36:39,260
I love it.

722
00:36:39,280 --> 00:36:40,300
Earth is a trajectory.

723
00:36:40,860 --> 00:36:46,720
It is a sequence of successive physical states, cooling magma, shifting continents, evolving

724
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:50,840
atmospheres that are similar enough from one millennium to the next that calling them

725
00:36:50,840 --> 00:36:57,220
all by the same single name, earth, is a highly useful compression over our short human timescales.

726
00:36:57,220 --> 00:36:58,000
Oh, man.

727
00:36:58,320 --> 00:37:03,220
Because my human lifespan is so incredibly short, the earth looks perfectly identical the day

728
00:37:03,220 --> 00:37:04,500
I am born and the day I die.

729
00:37:04,580 --> 00:37:06,280
So I just label it a noun to save time.

730
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:12,460
The name compresses the entire complex billion-year trajectory into a single point and then treats

731
00:37:12,460 --> 00:37:14,240
that point as a fundamental entity.

732
00:37:14,620 --> 00:37:16,480
Affliction calls this the noun fallacy.

733
00:37:16,640 --> 00:37:17,400
The noun fallacy.

734
00:37:17,600 --> 00:37:24,000
It is the tendency to mistake the grammatical fixity of a word for the physical fixity of

735
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:24,700
the thing named.

736
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:31,460
The moment we assign a noun to something, we implicitly assign its rate of change to zero.

737
00:37:31,800 --> 00:37:35,000
We commit a frozen process error at the very moment of naming.

738
00:37:35,180 --> 00:37:38,820
That is staggering because it means the noun isn't necessarily a lie.

739
00:37:38,940 --> 00:37:40,580
It's just a compression algorithm.

740
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:43,500
It is a ZIP file for reality.

741
00:37:43,740 --> 00:37:44,300
I love that.

742
00:37:44,380 --> 00:37:45,960
A ZIP file for reality.

743
00:37:46,080 --> 00:37:50,520
But it becomes incredibly misleading when our prediction horizon extends far enough that

744
00:37:50,520 --> 00:37:52,900
the thing is actually changing underneath the label.

745
00:37:52,900 --> 00:37:57,720
And nowhere is this more consequential than in how we forecast human institutions.

746
00:37:58,240 --> 00:37:59,760
This is Flyxion's prime example.

747
00:38:00,440 --> 00:38:04,700
Institutions organize the vast majority of human life, and they are named entities by

748
00:38:04,700 --> 00:38:05,220
definition.

749
00:38:05,720 --> 00:38:09,760
A government, a legal tradition, a massive corporation, a university.

750
00:38:10,120 --> 00:38:13,220
The text specifically uses Harvard University as an example.

751
00:38:13,480 --> 00:38:17,580
Think about an institution named in the 17th century, like Harvard, that still bears that

752
00:38:17,580 --> 00:38:19,320
exact same name in the 21st century.

753
00:38:19,600 --> 00:38:21,920
In what sense is it actually the same entity?

754
00:38:21,920 --> 00:38:26,440
Its personnel have been entirely replaced dozens of times over.

755
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:28,580
Its technologies are utterly different.

756
00:38:28,780 --> 00:38:34,300
Its cultural practices, its power structure, its geographic footprint, the very subjects it

757
00:38:34,300 --> 00:38:37,100
teaches all have completely transformed.

758
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:40,200
Literally, the only thing that has persisted is the word Harvard.

759
00:38:41,020 --> 00:38:45,060
If you sign a contract with Harvard today, you aren't signing a contract with an object.

760
00:38:45,060 --> 00:38:45,540
No.

761
00:38:45,540 --> 00:38:51,780
What persists is a trajectory of organizational activity that has been continuous enough in

762
00:38:51,780 --> 00:38:54,380
some attributes to maintain a shared legal name.

763
00:38:54,720 --> 00:38:54,760
Okay.

764
00:38:55,180 --> 00:39:00,300
But if you try to predict what Harvard will do in 50 years by extrapolating its past, and

765
00:39:00,300 --> 00:39:05,540
you treat it as a fixed object with stable, immutable properties, you are committing a massive

766
00:39:05,540 --> 00:39:06,980
frozen process error.

767
00:39:07,200 --> 00:39:08,640
Because it's not a fixed object.

768
00:39:08,640 --> 00:39:13,860
Harvard is a trajectory that includes internal mechanisms for massive institutional change on

769
00:39:13,860 --> 00:39:17,800
a timescale totally comparable to a 50-year forecasting horizon.

770
00:39:18,040 --> 00:39:21,480
What's fascinating here is how this applies to an entire culture's worldview.

771
00:39:22,160 --> 00:39:25,680
Flyxion says that language forms a collective frozen process commitment.

772
00:39:25,940 --> 00:39:26,520
It does.

773
00:39:26,520 --> 00:39:32,040
If I speak a language that is heavily skewed towards nouns, how does that change my forecasting

774
00:39:32,040 --> 00:39:34,680
compared to someone who speaks a language rich in verbs?

775
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:36,340
It is a profound observation.

776
00:39:37,340 --> 00:39:39,780
A language does not just passively describe the world.

777
00:39:40,200 --> 00:39:44,660
It actively shapes the default partition of what its speakers treat as background parameters

778
00:39:44,660 --> 00:39:46,940
and what they treat as dynamic variables.

779
00:39:47,100 --> 00:39:47,400
Okay.

780
00:39:47,880 --> 00:39:54,160
A language with a rich verb vocabulary for ecological processes, words that emphasize flow, succession,

781
00:39:54,360 --> 00:39:55,420
decay, and adaptation.

782
00:39:55,420 --> 00:40:01,340
But a sparse noun vocabulary for landscape features would generate a completely different

783
00:40:01,340 --> 00:40:06,980
worldview than a language that has a rigid, fixed noun for every hill, rock, and tree.

784
00:40:07,120 --> 00:40:12,340
If I have a specific noun for that specific hill, I inherently subconsciously assume that

785
00:40:12,340 --> 00:40:12,980
hill is prominent.

786
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:13,640
Exactly.

787
00:40:13,860 --> 00:40:18,720
If my language describes that area as earth rising and eroding, I am naturally primed to

788
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:19,620
see it as a trajectory.

789
00:40:20,140 --> 00:40:23,660
I am immune to the frozen process error because my grammar won't let me freeze it.

790
00:40:23,660 --> 00:40:26,600
Neither linguistic partition is absolute truth.

791
00:40:27,220 --> 00:40:29,680
Each is just useful for different prediction horizons.

792
00:40:30,240 --> 00:40:35,600
But the grammar of your language applies its default partition automatically without you

793
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:38,760
ever consciously running that timescale check we talked about.

794
00:40:38,900 --> 00:40:43,160
So every sentence you utter smuggles in a frozen process commitment?

795
00:40:43,440 --> 00:40:44,260
Yes, precisely.

796
00:40:44,260 --> 00:40:49,640
Which means part of the job of scientific revolutions, like plate tectonics, is to literally fight

797
00:40:49,640 --> 00:40:52,400
against the noun-induced default of our own brains.

798
00:40:53,180 --> 00:40:58,440
Wegener had to destroy the noun continent as a fixed feature and replace it with a verb,

799
00:40:58,680 --> 00:41:00,420
a process of drifting plates.

800
00:41:00,860 --> 00:41:03,640
He had to correct the noun fallacy at a planetary scale.

801
00:41:03,740 --> 00:41:08,400
And this requires immense cognitive effort because you are fighting your own vocabulary.

802
00:41:08,580 --> 00:41:09,980
It's exhausting just to think about.

803
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:14,540
Okay, this is blowing my mind, but it also leads me to a slightly terrifying realization.

804
00:41:14,860 --> 00:41:15,460
Uh-oh.

805
00:41:15,540 --> 00:41:20,980
If we freeze physical variables like orbits, and we freeze behavioral rules like economics,

806
00:41:21,320 --> 00:41:26,400
and we even freeze the very language we used to think, what is the ultimate limit of our

807
00:41:26,400 --> 00:41:26,720
freezing?

808
00:41:27,140 --> 00:41:29,200
Is there a final boss of prediction failure?

809
00:41:29,560 --> 00:41:30,020
There is.

810
00:41:30,660 --> 00:41:33,120
Infliction tackles it in section 7 of the monograph.

811
00:41:34,060 --> 00:41:35,720
Reachability and frozen futures.

812
00:41:35,900 --> 00:41:36,260
Okay.

813
00:41:36,740 --> 00:41:38,560
This is level 3 in the hierarchy of freezing.

814
00:41:38,560 --> 00:41:44,160
It is the deepest, most invisible, and most consequential error a modeler can make.

815
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:46,440
It is called possibility freezing.

816
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:48,020
Possibility freezing.

817
00:41:48,220 --> 00:41:50,780
We are literally freezing the future itself.

818
00:41:51,080 --> 00:41:53,960
We freeze the actual set of futures available to us.

819
00:41:54,840 --> 00:41:59,020
In dynamical systems theory, there is a concept called the reachable state space.

820
00:41:59,380 --> 00:42:00,760
Reachable state space.

821
00:42:00,880 --> 00:42:05,020
Simply put, from where we are right now, given the actions and resources currently available

822
00:42:05,020 --> 00:42:08,440
to us, what are all the possible states we could transition into?

823
00:42:08,560 --> 00:42:10,760
Like a menu of possible futures.

824
00:42:10,940 --> 00:42:15,240
If I'm at a restaurant, the reachable state space is whatever is printed on the menu.

825
00:42:15,240 --> 00:42:20,600
A frozen reachability error occurs when a model assumes that the menu of futures is eternally

826
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:21,080
fixed.

827
00:42:21,560 --> 00:42:26,020
It assumes the reachable set today will be the exact same reachable set 30 years from now.

828
00:42:26,060 --> 00:42:27,100
Which is never true.

829
00:42:27,280 --> 00:42:27,500
Never.

830
00:42:28,100 --> 00:42:32,500
Flyxion breaks down three distinct mechanisms by which reachable sets actually change over

831
00:42:32,500 --> 00:42:32,840
time.

832
00:42:32,840 --> 00:42:33,880
Let's walk through them.

833
00:42:34,040 --> 00:42:36,120
Number one, innovation expands the set.

834
00:42:36,460 --> 00:42:40,780
A technology that doesn't exist today isn't on the menu today, but it might be on the menu

835
00:42:40,780 --> 00:42:41,720
in 20 years.

836
00:42:42,280 --> 00:42:45,480
Flyxion points to Malthusian predictions as the classic example.

837
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:47,000
Thomas Malthus from the 1700s.

838
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:55,240
In 1798, Thomas Malthus famously predicted catastrophic global famine because human population growth

839
00:42:55,240 --> 00:42:57,580
would mathematically outpace food production.

840
00:42:58,320 --> 00:43:01,380
But Malthus didn't just miscalculate the birth rates.

841
00:43:02,200 --> 00:43:06,400
His deepest error was freezing the agricultural technology space.

842
00:43:06,940 --> 00:43:13,280
He treated the 1798 production methods as the permanent, unchangeable frontier of what was

843
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:14,200
physically possible.

844
00:43:14,200 --> 00:43:16,260
He couldn't foresee the industrial revolution.

845
00:43:16,460 --> 00:43:20,920
He couldn't foresee synthetic fertilizers or mechanized farming or genetically modified

846
00:43:20,920 --> 00:43:21,360
crops.

847
00:43:21,520 --> 00:43:22,700
He froze the menu.

848
00:43:22,900 --> 00:43:27,220
So his mathematical model literally could not imagine a future where humanity fed itself.

849
00:43:27,540 --> 00:43:30,100
The second way the menu changes is constraint relaxation.

850
00:43:31,160 --> 00:43:37,140
Legal prohibitions, physical bottlenecks, political barriers, these all restrict the menu of available

851
00:43:37,140 --> 00:43:37,640
actions.

852
00:43:37,920 --> 00:43:44,060
When a political revelation happens or a major social barrier falls, the reachable set expands

853
00:43:44,060 --> 00:43:44,560
dramatically.

854
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:50,880
A forecast that models the future within the current, fixed set of constraints will systematically

855
00:43:50,880 --> 00:43:52,020
miss that expansion.

856
00:43:52,780 --> 00:43:55,680
And the third way is the dark mirror of the first two.

857
00:43:56,360 --> 00:43:59,360
Erosion of capacity shrinks the reachable set.

858
00:43:59,760 --> 00:44:03,840
This is where the theory gets grim and highly relevant to modern policy.

859
00:44:03,840 --> 00:44:09,000
Degradation of physical infrastructure, loss of institutional knowledge, the depletion of

860
00:44:09,000 --> 00:44:13,740
a natural resource, or the accumulation of massive debt, whether financial debt or ecological

861
00:44:13,740 --> 00:44:14,100
debt.

862
00:44:14,180 --> 00:44:15,140
It shrinks your options.

863
00:44:15,520 --> 00:44:17,340
All of these reduce your available actions.

864
00:44:18,040 --> 00:44:23,660
If a long-term forecast ignores this erosion, it will wildly overestimate what the system will

865
00:44:23,660 --> 00:44:26,780
actually be capable of doing when that future horizon arrives.

866
00:44:26,780 --> 00:44:28,560
Let me test this with an example.

867
00:44:29,240 --> 00:44:33,780
If a city planner is forecasting traffic in 30 years, and they base it purely on today's

868
00:44:33,780 --> 00:44:37,500
road network, they aren't just getting the math of the population growth wrong.

869
00:44:37,980 --> 00:44:42,700
They are failing to imagine that the very definition of transportation might completely change.

870
00:44:42,960 --> 00:44:43,360
Exactly.

871
00:44:43,580 --> 00:44:45,780
They freeze the infrastructure network as a parameter.

872
00:44:46,600 --> 00:44:51,380
Over 30 years, that network will degrade, maybe some bridges collapse, or maybe autonomous

873
00:44:51,380 --> 00:44:54,160
vehicles, or entirely new transit modes emerge.

874
00:44:54,160 --> 00:44:59,320
The prediction error isn't about guessing the exact number of cars on a specific route.

875
00:44:59,720 --> 00:45:03,220
The error is freezing the space of routes that will even exist.

876
00:45:03,980 --> 00:45:08,980
And Flyxion connects this to a massive normative problem in how we run society.

877
00:45:09,800 --> 00:45:11,260
The admissibility problem.

878
00:45:11,720 --> 00:45:12,240
Admissibility?

879
00:45:12,340 --> 00:45:14,480
I've never heard that term used in this context.

880
00:45:14,620 --> 00:45:15,340
What does that mean?

881
00:45:15,560 --> 00:45:20,860
A trajectory is admissible if it does not shrink your reachable menu below what you need to survive

882
00:45:20,860 --> 00:45:21,820
and adapt in the future.

883
00:45:22,080 --> 00:45:23,100
Okay, walk me through that.

884
00:45:23,100 --> 00:45:26,220
Imagine a system that optimizes purely for present performance.

885
00:45:27,500 --> 00:45:30,520
Let's say a marathon runner wants to win the first mile of the race.

886
00:45:30,840 --> 00:45:35,940
So they sprint at top speed, burning through their body's finite supply of glycogen and physically

887
00:45:35,940 --> 00:45:37,080
tearing their muscle tissue.

888
00:45:37,260 --> 00:45:39,180
They look incredibly fast on mile one.

889
00:45:39,500 --> 00:45:40,780
Their model says they are winning.

890
00:45:41,200 --> 00:45:44,120
In their frozen future model, that plan looks optimal.

891
00:45:44,720 --> 00:45:48,340
But in reality, they are depleting their biological capacity.

892
00:45:48,740 --> 00:45:51,020
They are shrinking their reachable future volume.

893
00:45:51,020 --> 00:45:56,220
They are following a trajectory that is locally optimal but globally inadmissible.

894
00:45:56,860 --> 00:46:02,220
They reach mile 10 and they have zero options left to adapt because they consumed the biological

895
00:46:02,220 --> 00:46:04,020
capacity on which their future depended.

896
00:46:04,420 --> 00:46:08,060
The optimization in the spreadsheet literally destroys the real world future.

897
00:46:08,060 --> 00:46:12,720
We see corporations do this all the time, gutting research and development to maximize quarterly

898
00:46:12,720 --> 00:46:16,740
profits, looking great on paper but ensuring banknancy in a decade.

899
00:46:16,980 --> 00:46:17,820
Happens constantly.

900
00:46:17,960 --> 00:46:18,560
That is heavy.

901
00:46:19,080 --> 00:46:24,300
So with all these traps, variables drifting in space, human rules adapting to central banks,

902
00:46:24,520 --> 00:46:29,920
our language tricking us with nouns, and the very menu of possibilities expanding and shrinking,

903
00:46:29,920 --> 00:46:35,340
how does any system, whether it's a human being, a government, or an AI, actually survive?

904
00:46:35,720 --> 00:46:37,440
What is the antidote to all this freezing?

905
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:40,920
The antidote is what Flyxion outlines in the final chapter.

906
00:46:41,540 --> 00:46:44,020
True intelligence as unfreezing capacity.

907
00:46:44,640 --> 00:46:46,920
This is the culmination of the entire general theory.

908
00:46:47,620 --> 00:46:51,520
Flyxion completely redefines what adaptive intelligence actually is.

909
00:46:51,520 --> 00:46:54,940
So it's not just about having a high IQ or a fast supercomputer?

910
00:46:55,520 --> 00:46:56,060
Not at all.

911
00:46:56,140 --> 00:47:02,320
Being able to execute a complex mathematical model perfectly without revision is just competence.

912
00:47:02,800 --> 00:47:04,500
It is not intelligence in this framework.

913
00:47:05,140 --> 00:47:08,300
True to active intelligence is not about being competent within a model.

914
00:47:08,840 --> 00:47:14,940
It is the capacity to notice the model is wrong and the ability to step outside it and fix it.

915
00:47:15,060 --> 00:47:17,560
The text contrasts prediction with meta prediction.

916
00:47:17,660 --> 00:47:18,060
Exactly.

917
00:47:18,340 --> 00:47:20,400
A standard predictive system just runs the model.

918
00:47:20,400 --> 00:47:23,540
It assumes the parameters are fixed and churns out answers.

919
00:47:24,200 --> 00:47:27,320
A meta predictive system constantly monitors the parameters themselves.

920
00:47:27,460 --> 00:47:28,440
It's watching the watchers.

921
00:47:28,560 --> 00:47:30,340
It runs the timescale ratio in the background.

922
00:47:30,820 --> 00:47:33,960
It asks, are these background variables actually staying fixed?

923
00:47:34,340 --> 00:47:38,920
Is the epsilon ratio on this variable quietly growing as my time horizon extends?

924
00:47:39,300 --> 00:47:44,960
It's constantly checking the bucket of frozen variables to see if anything is secretly thawing out and starting to move.

925
00:47:45,220 --> 00:47:50,020
Unfreezing capacity is the ability to detect when a frozen parameter is evolving too fast.

926
00:47:50,020 --> 00:47:53,580
And then actively moving it into the dynamic state category.

927
00:47:54,020 --> 00:47:59,200
Updating the very ontology of the model before the prediction error becomes catastrophic.

928
00:47:59,200 --> 00:48:00,740
So what does this all mean?

929
00:48:01,060 --> 00:48:05,180
It means you can be absolutely brilliant at executing a mathematical model.

930
00:48:05,180 --> 00:48:17,040
Like you can calculate the sun's expansion and the tidal drag with flawless precision, but you utterly lack adaptive intelligence if you never step back and question the structural commitments of the model itself.

931
00:48:17,040 --> 00:48:17,440
Right.

932
00:48:17,440 --> 00:48:20,480
If you never ask, hey, does the earth move too?

933
00:48:20,480 --> 00:48:28,140
A system with low unfreezing capacity will perform amazingly well as long as the world happens to perfectly match its frozen assumptions.

934
00:48:28,840 --> 00:48:37,980
But as the real world inevitably diverges, that system will fail systematically, accumulating massive errors in precisely the blind spots it refuses to look at.

935
00:48:37,980 --> 00:48:38,420
Wow.

936
00:48:38,420 --> 00:48:45,720
And Flyxion takes this one step further in the epilogue, tying it to a broader philosophical program called the ecology of distinctions.

937
00:48:46,200 --> 00:48:48,180
The deepest unfreezing of them all.

938
00:48:48,740 --> 00:48:55,840
The deepest unfreezing is realizing that the boundary between state and parameter is not a law of nature.

939
00:48:55,840 --> 00:49:01,240
The partition itself is a fluid variable that requires constant maintenance and checking.

940
00:49:01,600 --> 00:49:01,720
Oh.

941
00:49:02,000 --> 00:49:06,640
A truly intelligent civilization treats its own ontology as provisional.

942
00:49:07,000 --> 00:49:11,640
It preserves its future capacity to revise its fundamental distinctions.

943
00:49:12,000 --> 00:49:16,960
We have covered an immense amount of ground today, and I want to make sure this all locks in for you listening.

944
00:49:17,400 --> 00:49:19,680
Let's do a rapid fire summary to bring this all home.

945
00:49:19,860 --> 00:49:20,340
Sounds good.

946
00:49:20,340 --> 00:49:31,300
We started with the textbook Fate of the Earth and the Sun, and realized that treating the Earth's orbit as a frozen process changes the math from definitely engulfed to maybe surviving.

947
00:49:31,540 --> 00:49:37,940
We introduced the timescale ratio, epsilon, which tells us exactly when a mathematical shortcut becomes a fatal emission.

948
00:49:37,940 --> 00:49:50,180
We looked at the history of science as a sequence of moving parameters from Darwin unfreezing the static concept of species, to Wegener unfreezing the continents, to Hubble unfreezing the container of the universe itself.

949
00:49:50,440 --> 00:49:57,100
And we explored how the Lucas critique proves human behavior adapts to the very forecasts we make about it.

950
00:49:57,100 --> 00:50:08,680
We unpacked the noun fallacy, revealing how language tricks us into treating compressed trajectories as permanent objects, leading us to freeze the menu of the future itself through reachability errors.

951
00:50:08,840 --> 00:50:19,540
And finally, we redefined true intelligence as unfreezing capacity, the meta-awareness, to catch your own model failing and restructure reality before it's too late.

952
00:50:19,660 --> 00:50:25,080
It is a stunningly cohesive theory of why we get the future wrong and how we can learn to get it right.

953
00:50:25,300 --> 00:50:25,900
It really is.

954
00:50:25,900 --> 00:50:31,660
I want to end by taking all this incredible astrophysics and economic theory and bringing it right to your doorstep.

955
00:50:32,080 --> 00:50:35,560
Think about your own life for a second, your career, your relationships.

956
00:50:36,140 --> 00:50:39,380
What are the nouns in your life that you are treating as fixed parameters?

957
00:50:39,860 --> 00:50:44,140
What is evolving right under your nose that you have silently set to zero by omission?

958
00:50:44,840 --> 00:50:46,580
The Harvard example is so poignant here.

959
00:50:46,680 --> 00:50:51,780
If an institution is just a trajectory compressed into a noun, then so is a person.

960
00:50:51,780 --> 00:50:55,240
If Harvard is just a trajectory, then so is your identity.

961
00:50:55,900 --> 00:51:02,180
When you try to forecast where you will be in 10 years, are you committing a frozen process error on yourself?

962
00:51:02,340 --> 00:51:03,300
That's a great question.

963
00:51:03,560 --> 00:51:10,920
Are you projecting your future, assuming your current desires, your current fears, and your current capacities are fixed parameters?

964
00:51:11,080 --> 00:51:12,000
Because they aren't.

965
00:51:12,100 --> 00:51:12,920
They are trajectories.

966
00:51:12,960 --> 00:51:14,000
They're constantly moving.

967
00:51:14,000 --> 00:51:19,720
The future is often determined not by the variables we obsessively track, but by the trajectories we forget to notice.

968
00:51:20,060 --> 00:51:22,300
Maybe it's time to check your own timescale ratio.

969
00:51:22,300 --> 00:51:24,520
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

